      "status": "draft",
      "stem": "At $298\\ \\mathrm{K}$, the limiting molar conductivity of electrolytes $\\mathrm{\\Lambda^\\circ}$ for $\\mathrm{NaCl}$, $\\mathrm{HCl}$ and $\\mathrm{CH_3COONa}$ are $126.4$, $425.9$ and $91.0\\ \\mathrm{S\\ cm^2\\ mol^{-1}}$ respectively. The limiting molar conductivity of $\\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$ (in $\\mathrm{S\\ cm^2\\ mol^{-1}}$) is:",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "content": "$390.5$",
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "content": "$425.5$",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "content": "$290.5$",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "content": "$541.3$",
          "is_correct": false
        }
      ],
      "solution": "According to Kohlrausch's law:\n$\\mathrm{\\Lambda^\\circ(CH_3COOH) = \\Lambda^\\circ(CH_3COONa) + \\Lambda^\\circ(HCl) - \\Lambda^\\circ(NaCl)}$\n$= 91.0 + 425.9 - 126.4 = 390.5\\ \\mathrm{S\\ cm^2\\ mol^{-1}}$."
    },
    {
